[Music]  an estimated 152 million children are  being put to work around the world  hard manual labor in minds in fields  on family farms  in africa one in five kids are involved  in child labour  girls are especially vulnerable the  number of children at work is said to  dramatically worsen because of the  pandemic  as it wreaks havoc on family incomes  many parents could put their kids to  work  more than a billion children in 130  countries have been affected by school  closures  and child labour has risen in line as  school gates  banks shut the organization for economic  cooperation and development has told dw  that children are paying the largest  price in this pandemic  director for education and skills under  isla praises the countries that have  managed to keep schools open  we need to be aware of the high social  costs that school closures have  for children and that's why you know i  value countries you know like  france and italy and uh to some extent  even spain that you know despite very  difficult pandemic  contexts have given education absolute  priority  india allowed schools to reopen in  october but some children will only head  back now  due to a staggered resumption of classes  many children in india's villages may  not return at all  we traveled to uttar pradesh  asad rahman started apprenticing six  weeks ago at this furniture shop  in a village in uttar pradesh the shy 12  year old has been half-heartedly  listening to instructions  he's clearly not enjoying it  i want to go back to school meet my  friends  i don't want to work here but he doesn't  really have an  option nor does his younger brother  ashjad  who joined him here a few days ago  both are following their father's  instructions  abdul rahman is out of work himself  with schools closed for nearly a year  now due to the coronavirus  he has given up hope their education  will secure them work  rahman says he's only looking out for  their future  schools have been closed they were  studying they not  school available the state government  has just announced that junior classes  are set to resume  but assad and ashjad may need to stay at  work to support their family  high school classes resumed back in  october  but children younger than 15 years have  hardly studied for the better part of a  year  at best they while away their time  playing with their friends  at worst they are forced into labor or  are married off  kuthuba deen chaudhary has worried  endlessly about this  especially about the 400 children who  attended his school  his class is much smaller now he just  helps his own daughters with their  studies  private schools like chaudharys educate  close to half of india's children  often for very little money now  his own school survival is at stake  chaudhary waved his 100 rupee or  approximately one euro monthly fee  during the lockdown he still fears that  many can't afford to continue sending  their children  most of the children are accompanying  their parents to work  i fear 70 of them will drop out  we try to appeal to the parents to keep  educating their kids  but they just say if we don't have money  how are we supposed to send our children  to school  the lack of resources also rules out  online education  chaudhary says if parents  already can't afford a nominal fee how  can they pay for multiple smartphones  for all the children at home  education has not been a priority in his  village he says  and it had been an uphill battle even  before the lockdown hit  chaudhary is excited to see the children  again soon  but he is also realistic he knows  that even if just a quarter of them show  up  he can count it as a victory  if at idris is an expert on child labor  in south asia and joins us from the  university of birmingham in the uk  what's causing this huge jump in child  labor during the pandemic  um well thank you for having me um i  think it's all the  effects of the pandemic are also drivers  of child labour so the pandemic is  causing an economic shock  across the world but it's particularly  felt in developing countries it's  causing a rise in poverty  it's closing schools which traditionally  protect children from  child labour it's causing even less  enforcement of child labor laws and  regulation  and it's meant that cs civil society  organizations can't protect children and  monitor them  so for all those reasons a pandemic is  leading to a rise  in child labour what about the checks  and balances you mentioned schools there  and regulations but  have are the checks and balances simply  gone out the window in this pandemic  well checks and balances in developing  countries tend to be weak anyway because  of limited  government capacity so as i said  employers now have more of a free reign  to to use children and to exploit them  because  governments for attention is focused on  on dealing with the pandemic and civil  society organizations are not able to  carry out  the protective role that they normally  would but schools i think schools are  the real significant  sort of protector of children and that  has been removed when you take kids out  of schools  in developing countries you greatly um  increase their risk of expo  of being forced into into work just  briefly of course sending these kids  into work rather than to school  limits their prospects what what does it  do what's the impact  on the world on the global economy  well it's it's kind of like i i think  about it is if you sort of throw stone  in water and you see ripple effects  so child labour has the same kind of  ripple effect which  which which you know spreads outward so  the child themselves they're affected  because they can't  study they're exposed to physical labor  often physical abuse verbal abuse even  sexual abuse  but in the long term that child then  grows up  to be in low paid work to be unemployed  um to be um doing hazardous work  and critically child laborers grow up  into adults who then send their own  children out to work  and so you get this intergenerational  cycle of poverty child labour property  child labor  and that impacts collectively as a  society and as a country that  greatly hampers economic growth and  development  and that then poverty under development  those are both  major contributory factors to all kinds  of other problems  like armed violence conflict  radicalization extremism  illegal trafficking illegal migration  human trafficking and those have  consequences for everyone  but i think you know that the point i  really want to stress is that  you know we we need to address people in  the developed world need to address this  problem  not just because down the road it will  come back to hit  us here but because we have a moral  responsibility to do so these are  children  children have the right to you know a  future  and to be secure and safe and have  opportunities and we all need to  support them in doing that and as you  pointed out there that the longer term  effects are even longer than than one  would expect if an idris  thank you very much for joining us today  thank you for having me now it's that  part of the show where derek williams  fields your questions on the coronavirus  so what do we currently know about when  the pandemic actually started  more than we did at this time last year  but not a  whole lot more a recent who mission that  spent  weeks in china investigating the origins  of the pandemic  came to a few unsurprising conclusions  the first was that its members agreed  that sars  cov2 had originated in bats  which other experts have more or less  been saying the whole time  um the second was that it believed the  virus  had jumped to humans via an unidentified  second species which is more information  that's  pretty old hat although they did say  that they suspected  it might be a small mammal which was a  specification that was  sort of new i guess the mission also  said it considered it  extremely unlikely that sars kovi ii  had somehow escaped from a wuhan lab  that specializes in bat viruses although  that assessment remains pretty  controversial  really for me the most interesting  takeaway  was this we can also  agree that we have  found evidence of wider circulation of  the virus  in in december it was not just  only a cluster outbreak  in the juanan market but the virus was  also circulated  outside of the markets that's new um  up until now the working hypothesis was  that covet 19  first struck at a wet market in wuhan  around the beginning of december of 2019  but  if it was already in wider circulation  in the city at that point  that means a couple of things first that  the wet market wasn't ground zero  and then of course that we have to push  back the timeline  of the original zoonotic jump a while um  although a few studies looking at how  the virus has evolved  say the earliest date their models  predict for a possible  jump is october of 2019.  so we learned something from the who  experts  maybe not all that earth-shaking but  interesting and and to be fair uh no one  expected the mission to figure out  everything about the pandemic's origins  in a couple of weeks um the wheels of  this kind of scientific  detective work uh turn very slowly  and as we heard earlier the pandemic has  put the welfare of children around the  world  in peril  the smiles behind the masks speak  volumes and education is a powerful  weapon to secure their futures  germany's daycare centers and elementary  schools are open again after two months  wales youngest pupils are returning in  the first step towards reopening schools  and in israel where vaccination rates  are high elementary school and the last  two years of high school classes have  resumed  in towns with cadentian rates under  control  thanks for watching stay safe and see  you again soon

